#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151874 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "287a493f-e988-5cef-b0d0-4205d1eaf74e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6313" :question/problem "Find a definite integral with respect to $\\theta$ that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} (y^2-x) \\, \\textrm ds$, where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r = \\cos{\\theta}\\:$ for $0 \\le \\theta \\le\\dfrac{\\pi}4$." :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,\n$$\n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ \\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} θ, \\\\\ny &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ \\cdot \\sin θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ\\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\cos^2\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\cos\\theta \\sin{\\theta}\n$$\n\n, where\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}4\n$$\n\nNow, if\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =y^2-x\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ) &= (\\cos θ\\sin θ)^{2} - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} θ\\sin^{2} θ - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} θ(\\sin^{2} θ - 1) \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} θ(-\\cos^{2} θ) \\\\\n&=-\\cos^{4} θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dr}{dθ} &= \\frac{d}{dθ}(\\cos θ) =-\\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ &= \\sqrt{(\\cos θ)^{2} + (-\\sin θ)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)(y^{2} - x)ds &= ∫_{0}^{π/4}f(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ)\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/4} - \\cos^{4} θ \\cdot 1 \\cdot dθ \\\\\n&= - ∫_{0}^{π/4}\\cos^{4} θdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151875 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9964be68-9e22-5d98-b0f8-ae19b6e94c1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Polar Form"}] :question/id #uuid "63aa09fb-9839-5f73-9f98-2eb9784ead43" :question/math-academy-id "e-6314" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}$ along the polar curve $r=1$ for $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac\\pi 2$." :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,\n$$\n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r\\cos θ = \\cos θ, \\\\\ny &= r\\sin θ = \\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\n, where\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}2\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ) &= \\frac{\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ}{\\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= \\csc^{2} θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dr}{dθ} &= \\frac{d}{dθ}(1) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ = \\sqrt{1^{2} + 0^{2}} = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)\\frac{x^{2} + y^{2}}{y^{2}}ds &= ∫_{π/6}^{π/2}f(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ)\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/6}^{π/2}\\csc^{2} θ \\cdot 1 \\cdot dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/6}^{π/2}\\csc^{2} θdθ \\\\\n&=-\\cot θ \\mid _{π/6}^{π/2} \\\\\n&= - (0 - \\sqrt{3}) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151876 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6e8ead-8186-5afe-bd66-d11c55b65dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "00b29cf2-6a73-5fd6-b8bd-04ba8c5f1a46" :question/math-academy-id "e-6315" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C} xy(1-x^2) \\,\\textrm{d}s$, where $C$ is the polar curve $r=\\cos{\\theta}\\:$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,\n$$\n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ \\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} θ, \\\\\ny &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ \\cdot \\sin θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ\\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\cos^2\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\n, where\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, if\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = xy(1-x^2)\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ) &= \\cos^{2} θ \\cdot \\cos θ\\sin θ(1 - (\\cos^{2} θ)^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\cos^{3} θ\\sin θ(1 - \\cos^{4} θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dr}{dθ} = \\frac{d}{dθ}(\\cos θ) =-\\sin θ\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ &= \\sqrt{(\\cos θ)^{2} + (-\\sin θ)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)xy(1 - x^{2})ds &= ∫_{0}^{π/2}f(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ)\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos^{3} θ\\sin θ(1 - \\cos^{4} θ) \\cdot 1 \\cdot dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos^{3} θ\\sin θ(1 - \\cos^{4} θ)dθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u=-\\sin\\theta\\,\\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos^{3} θ\\sin θ(1 - \\cos^{4} θ)dθ &= ∫_{1}^{0} - u^{3}(1 - u^{4})du \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(u^{3} - u^{7})du \\\\\n&= [\\frac{u^{4}}{4} - \\frac{u^{8}}{8}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{8}) - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9964be68-9e22-5d98-b0f8-ae19b6e94c1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6b802fa-d788-59f1-b76a-819af8a17558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150007} {:answer/id #uuid "c28214c3-8b3c-5fe8-976a-e39d16b950cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150008} {:answer/id #uuid "e119b31e-7c2a-50d7-bd3a-623983958a9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150009} {:answer/id #uuid "e99ae455-f0b2-52a1-8186-86e4eca9cc4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150010} {:answer/id #uuid "712e3761-3923-55c5-b584-a8d98acf247e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "712e3761-3923-55c5-b584-a8d98acf247e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150011} :answer-field/id #uuid "872863e2-3052-56ee-bce6-3421ecfba5f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150006}] :question/id #uuid "e836460c-d649-52c9-8ad9-0a85310c1949" :question/math-academy-id "q-41188" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $∫_(C)(x + y)ds$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r = 2\\sqrt{\\cos 2θ}$ for $-\\frac{π}{4} \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{4}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aab4a5fd-cfc5-58df-92d8-aec15e4d2809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8∫_{0}^{π}\\sin^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150877} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6ed9db-99f8-52ae-81b5-c5a5d436e229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8∫_{0}^{π}\\cos θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150878} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4c1022-42b4-5792-a22a-f9bbfb3bffe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{0}^{π}\\cos^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150879} {:answer/id #uuid "7c787b58-4187-5b65-8fb3-c644d72ea171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2∫_{0}^{π}\\sin^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150880} {:answer/id #uuid "7f8520f8-9f38-54e7-aec2-0ad39c628b23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2∫_{0}^{π}\\sin θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aab4a5fd-cfc5-58df-92d8-aec15e4d2809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8∫_{0}^{π}\\sin^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150877} :answer-field/id #uuid "30a876f0-c14b-5ea6-be63-7b076908dc79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150876}] :question/id #uuid "aefe264e-7dab-56be-82be-12f2dc01af9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76345" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $∫_(C)(x^{2} + y^{2})ds$, where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r = 2\\sin θ$ for $0 \\le θ \\le π$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6e8ead-8186-5afe-bd66-d11c55b65dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86a7d9dd-d626-507e-b787-091b9f8e253c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(e^{2} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150883} {:answer/id #uuid "9be506db-1e12-5261-b7c6-694430a58478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(e - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150884} {:answer/id #uuid "e01eba0a-4402-56b8-ae73-f1cbc0b462a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(e^{π/2} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150885} {:answer/id #uuid "15949eb5-dbd3-5184-9f31-2d3810d33c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(e^{3} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150886} {:answer/id #uuid "68a66aaf-e198-5aff-93b6-da13090c52b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{π/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15949eb5-dbd3-5184-9f31-2d3810d33c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(e^{3} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150886} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce57c0ff-9f96-5467-ae19-ebb868702961" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150882}] :question/id #uuid "55dd4f9a-c59d-565d-9412-8e04ab857f89" :question/math-academy-id "q-76347" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x, y) = xe^{y}$ along the polar curve $r = 3$ for $0 \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed82c56f-e330-5ca4-8b49-1dcb4b9de09e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}∫_{- π/4}^{π/4}\\sin θ\\cos θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150895} {:answer/id #uuid "2032b8fb-1b5b-5b74-bcea-d98fa0390bfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{- π/4}^{π/4}\\sin θ\\cos θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150896} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad46fdb-c96f-5157-8892-c4a6d6621f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}∫_{- π/4}^{π/4}\\sin θ\\cos^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150897} {:answer/id #uuid "7305f421-5cae-5a64-b026-17046998fdce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2∫_{- π/4}^{π/4}\\sin^{2} θ\\cos θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150898} {:answer/id #uuid "7b42e790-2c33-55a6-ad34-50392b9ee23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{- π/4}^{π/4}\\sin θ\\cos^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b42e790-2c33-55a6-ad34-50392b9ee23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{- π/4}^{π/4}\\sin θ\\cos^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150899} :answer-field/id #uuid "b871ac13-c84c-5e91-887e-b02d400b729e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150894}] :question/id #uuid "fda4f6af-c917-5961-9062-323f1cdf3aeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-76355" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $∫_(C)x^{2}yds$, where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r = \\sqrt{2}$ for $-\\frac{π}{4} \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{4}$?"}]}
